    "chest": 34.8,
    "back": 36.2,
    "legs": 29
  },
  "strengthIndex": {},
  "routineAdherence": {
    "followedRoutines": [],
    "missedRoutines": []
  }
}

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Solid training week with balanced effort across all major muscle groups.",
  "summary": "You logged a complete training week with 12 sets distributed evenly across chest, back, and legs, accumulating 5520 kg total tonnage. Your volume split is well-balanced, with back slightly leading at 36.2 percent, chest at 34.8 percent, and legs at 29 percent. At 76.7 kg of tonnage per kilogram of bodyweight, you're maintaining a respectable training density for a male lifter of your age and build with a healthy BMI of 23.5. The consistency suggests you're adhering to your routine and keeping momentum going on your muscle-building goal.",
  "progressiveOverload": "\n<parameter name=\"status\">insufficient_data",
  "notes": "No strength index data was recorded this period, so I cannot assess whether your main lifts are progressing in weight or reps. To evaluate progressive overload effectively next period, log your lift performance metrics such as weight, reps, or RPE for your key compound movements like bench press, squat, and deadlift variants. This will give us concrete evidence of whether you are steadily building strength and advancing toward your muscle-building goal."
}
